both are used to sort objects based on its data members.



Difference Between Comparator and Comparable
Comparable:
Used to define the natural ordering of objects.
The class itself implements the Comparable interface.
Requires overriding the compareTo method.
Example: Sorting based on a single property (e.g., age).
    object1.compareTo(object2);



Comparator:
Used to define custom ordering of objects.
A separate class or lambda expression implements the Comparator interface.
Requires overriding the compare method.
Example: Sorting based on multiple properties (e.g., name, age).
<hr></hr>
Example Code
      compare(T o1, T o2);

      in our pojo to compare using data members.

        Comparator<Person> comparator = new Comparator<Person>() {
            @Override
            public int compare(Person p1, Person p2) {
                return p1.getAge() - p2.getAge(); // Compare based on age
            }
        };